  "query_preview": "..A cut off low centered over northern Arizona \ntoday, will move to the north and east through tonight. This system \nhas been keeping cooler temperatures across the region with dry air \nstarting to fill in from west to east. However, moisture continues \nto linger, especially across eastern Arizona, where isolated showers \nand thunderstorms are expected through this afternoon. Rainfall \namounts look to be light though a few heavier showers are possible. \n\nFor Monday through the end of the week...The low lifts out of the \nregion and the flow becomes more zonal across the area. A couple of \ntroughs look to pass to the north of the region through the week but \nremain far enough away that we see no impacts from them. Warming \ntemperatures are expected with drier conditions returning to the \nregion. This pattern looks to hold through about midweek, then we \nstart to see troughing develop to the west. The trough starts to dig \ndown from the Pacific Northwest but won't move through the region \nuntil the weekend/early next week. Meanwhile, the increasing \nsoutherly flow will allow for tropical/sub-tropical moisture to be \npulled back into the Desert Southwest. Models are in fair agreement \non the low tracking through the Great Basin next weekend, but \nseveral waves moving through the flow will move across Arizona and \nbring chances for showers and thunderstorms from Thursday onward. \nAdditional rainfall on our already saturated grounds may cause new \nflooding concerns. Definitely a system we want to keep an eye on as \nwe go through the week. Stay tuned!",
